### **Overall Detailed Summary**

This recording captures a data science working session where the team reviews their baseline machine learning models and makes a critical discovery about a severe flaw in their dataset. 

At the start of the meeting, the team discusses how they handled null values and feature engineering, with some members sharing that they built a baseline model achieving an R-squared of ~31.4% and an Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of ~33 days. The project lead encourages the team to treat the modeling phase like a "Kaggle competition," where each data scientist works independently to see who can engineer the best features and present the most accurate model.

However, as they review the model targets, they realize a major error in their underlying assumptions. The team was using the "Days Due" column as their target variable to predict when a client would pay an invoice. They suddenly realize that they do not actually have the true "Payment Date" (the date the invoice was actually cleared). The "Days Due" column merely shows how many days overdue an invoice is *as of the report's cut-off date*, which is useless for training a model to predict actual payment behavior. 

To work around this missing "Payment Date," the team brainstorms a clever proxy metric: tracking the lifecycle of an invoice across the monthly data dumps. If an invoice appears in the system in April but disappears from the records in December, they can mathematically deduce that it took approximately 8 months for the client to clear the payment. 

Finally, the team uncovers another anomaly: out of roughly 150,000 unique invoices, about 62 invoice IDs have duplicate rows tied to completely different business segments and legal entities. They assign a colleague to investigate if a single invoice can legitimately belong to multiple segments before they adjourn for another meeting.

